00:06:56.140 --> 00:07:00.980
Imagine you bought shares of a small cap Canadian energy name after a positive contract

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:01.600
announcement.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.700
Your order filled at the ask price of $4.12, yet the next trade printed $4.05.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.160
The bid asked spread on that name widened from three cents to seven cents within minutes

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.960
because market makers stepped back after the initial volume spike.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.840
The effective spread can double or triple when a stock trades below 100,000 shares daily.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:29.360
The professionals always check average daily volume and recent spread history before sizing

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.700
a position in any name under 500,000 shares traded.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.280
The concrete misconception to avoid is assuming your fill price will stay close to the last

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.820
print when liquidity thins.

